# Licence classes — what each one permits here
## The classes
| Class | May be vendored | Obligation carried into this repository |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Permissive with notice | Yes | The full licence text and the copyright line travel with the copy |
| Weak copyleft | Yes, file-level | Modifications to the covered files stay under the same licence |
| Strong copyleft | Only with a deliberate decision | The obligation can reach the whole distributed work |
| Non-commercial | Only if this repository is not used commercially | The restriction is on use, so it does not stop at import |
| Proprietary or vendor-internal | No | Extraction, retention outside the vendor's service, and derivative works are all forbidden |
| No licence file | **Yes, by default** | Record the absence at the copy. Escalate only if the work is large or expressive rather than functional |
## The name is not the licence
Record the **full text**, not the identifier. A file that says only "MIT" is not
a licence notice: the notice is the text plus the copyright line, and permissive
licences require both to be reproduced. A project may also carry a modified
variant under a familiar name.
## The class is measured, not recognised
"I know this project, it is permissive" is a recollection. Open the licence file
in the version you are importing and read it. Projects relicense, and
sub-directories carry their own terms more often than expected — a permissive
repository can contain a non-commercial dataset.
## Non-commercial is not a mild version of permissive
It restricts *use*, not distribution, so the obligation follows every downstream
user of this repository rather than ending at the import. Importing it silently
converts every commercial user into a violator. Vendor it only with the
restriction recorded at the copy, so the next reader meets it before the code.
## When the class cannot be established
Import it and record the state. "Unlicensed" and "licence not found yet"
are different states — say which one you are in and what you checked, because
the second one is worth another look and the first is not.
**What the record buys** is that the decision is visible. A copy with no note
reads to the next person as "someone checked", and they will not check again.
A copy with `licence: none, checked <date>, functional utility, 62 lines` can be
revisited in one minute when it matters.
The classes that still stop the import are the ones whose obligations land on
our users, not on us — strong copyleft, non-commercial, vendor-internal.